{
  "name": "Lead Qualification",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## What this does\n\nSeven website enquiries off one contact form on one morning.\n\n**Two are hot** and sales hears about them within the minute. **One is warm** and\ngets an acknowledgement, not a phone call. **Two are not a fit** - one is an\nagency selling to us, one is somebody after a job. **One needs a person to\ndecide.** **One never reaches the model at all.**\n\nEvery one of the seven ends up on a list, including the ones nothing was done\nabout. A lead that quietly evaporates is the failure this whole design is built\nagainst - and the bottom of the canvas is the half that catches the ones that\nevaporate for a reason nobody predicted.",
        "height": 340,
        "width": 440,
        "color": 4
      },
      "id": "f1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
      "name": "Note 1",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -2360,
        -560
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## The dropdowns say how big. The message says whether it is real.\n\nThis is the sentence to open the interview with, because it is where most\nversions of this workflow go wrong in both directions.\n\n**Budget and timeline are dropdowns.** They do not need a model - a model that\nreads \"Over £50,000\" correctly 97 times out of 100 misfiles three leads in every\nhundred, and nobody notices for a month. So `Score the Lead` is a Set node doing\narithmetic on the form's own fields, and it is right every time.\n\n**The free-text box does need a model, and L-03 is why.** An agency selling lead\ngeneration ticked *Over £50,000* and *Within a month*, because that is how you\nget attention. Every number on that form says drop everything. Only the words\nsay it is a pitch. Arithmetic alone would have put a salesperson on the phone to\na salesperson.\n\n**So the model categorises and never scores.** It answers one question - what\nkind of message is this - and hands back the words it read that from. A Set node\nand a Switch decide what happens. That ordering is the design, and it is the\nthing to defend when they ask.",
        "height": 460,
        "width": 520,
        "color": 3
      },
      "id": "f1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002",
      "name": "Note 2",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -1890,
        -560
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Sales is told before the CRM is written to\n\nLook at the order on the Hot lane: **notify, draft, then write the record.**\nThat is deliberate and it is worth saying out loud.\n\nIf the CRM is unreachable at 4pm on a Friday, the version that writes first\nnever gets as far as the notification, and the best lead of the week sits in a\nfailed execution nobody opens until Monday. This way the rep already has it.\n\n**In exchange the Slack message cannot carry a link to the record** - it carries\nthe enquiry itself, which is what the rep actually needs to make the call.",
        "height": 300,
        "width": 420,
        "color": 6
      },
      "id": "f1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003",
      "name": "Note 3",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -1350,
        -560
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Two ways a run breaks, and they are not the same failure\n\n**One lead fails.** `Create or Update the Contact` is set to try three times, and\nthen to send that lead out of its **second output** instead of stopping. The\nother six carry on. You still have the lead, so the log gets the name and the\nemail and somebody can re-run it.\n\n**The whole run dies.** `When the Whole Run Fails` catches anything else -\nincluding the things nobody predicted. Look at what n8n hands it: the workflow,\nthe node, the message, and a link. **And no trace of whose lead it was.** That is\nwhy the log row says *open the execution* - a person gets there in thirty\nseconds, which is thirty seconds better than never knowing.\n\n**The point of both, in one line for the client:** they find out from the log,\nnot from the customer.\n\n**The setup step everybody misses:** an Error Trigger does nothing until a\nworkflow is pointed at it. After importing, open **Settings → Error Workflow**\non this workflow and choose *this same workflow*. Skip that and the bottom half\nof this canvas is decoration.",
        "height": 480,
        "width": 500,
        "color": 5
      },
      "id": "f1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000004",
      "name": "Note 4",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -900,
        -560
      ]
    },
    {
